# These fixtures exist to test BYTES. Half of them carry a BOM, a bare CR, a
# CRLF pair, a cp1252 high byte, or an embedded NUL — every one of which git
# would be entitled to "helpfully" normalise on checkout or commit under
# core.autocrlf / text=auto. `-text` turns all of that off: what is committed is
# what lands on disk, on every platform.
#
# Without this line the corpus silently degrades into a set of ordinary LF text
# files on someone else's machine and the encoding tests pass for the wrong
# reason. tests/test_intake_adversarial.py asserts the committed bytes still
# match the generator, so a normalising checkout fails loudly instead.
* -text
